      "seed": 84464344,
      "version": 522,
      "versionNonce": 671411880,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": null,
      "updated": 1785905518803,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "Static Methods\n==============\n\n1. Static methods are methods declared using the `static` keyword.\n\n2. Static methods belong to the class, not to any object.\n\n3. Static methods can be called using the class name without creating an object.\n\n4. It is a good practice to declare a method as `static` in the following cases:\n\n   --> If the method accesses only static data.\n       Example: Accessing `nextId` in the `Emp` class.\n\n   --> If the method works only on its arguments (parameters)\n       and does not use any instance variables or instance methods.\n\n5. A static method cannot directly access instance (non-static) variables or call \ninstance methods because they belong to objects.\n\n6. A static method can directly access static variables and \ncall other static methods.\n\n7. The `this` and `super` keywords cannot be used inside \na static method because they refer to objects.\n\n8. Static methods are useful for utility/helper methods that \ndo not depend on an object's state.",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "Static Methods\n==============\n\n1. Static methods are methods declared using the `static` keyword.\n\n2. Static methods belong to the class, not to any object.\n\n3. Static methods can be called using the class name without creating an object.\n\n4. It is a good practice to declare a method as `static` in the following cases:\n\n   --> If the method accesses only static data.\n       Example: Accessing `nextId` in the `Emp` class.\n\n   --> If the method works only on its arguments (parameters)\n       and does not use any instance variables or instance methods.\n\n5. A static method cannot directly access instance (non-static) variables or call \ninstance methods because they belong to objects.\n\n6. A static method can directly access static variables and \ncall other static methods.\n\n7. The `this` and `super` keywords cannot be used inside \na static method because they refer to objects.\n\n8. Static methods are useful for utility/helper methods that \ndo not depend on an object's state.",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
    {
      "id": "jua56AzaYnp6NECLnfDww",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 254,
